;\n```\n"""'}], 'response': 'Step 1: The SQL query rewrite rule applies when there are functions or operations within the SELECT, WHERE, JOIN conditions, or any part of the query that is executed multiple times for the same row. Upon examining the given SQL query, we can identify the following segments that match this condition:\n\n* MIN(n.name) in the SELECT clause\n* n.name LIKE \'X%\' in the WHERE clause\n\nThese segments involve functions (MIN and LIKE) that are repeated or called multiple times within the query.\n\nStep 2:\nQuery Rewrite 1: """The SQL rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by moving the repeated function calls outside of loops, if applicable. In this case, we can simplify the MIN(n.name) calculations in the SELECT clause by applying the transformation.\n\nOriginal Query:\n```sql\nSELECT MIN(n.name) AS member_in_charnamed_movie,\n       MIN(n.name) AS a1\nFROM ...\n```\n\nTransformed Query:\n```sql\nWITH PreCalculated AS (\n  SELECT MIN(n.name) as min_name\n  FROM name AS n\n)\nSELECT PC.min_name AS member_in_charnamed_movie, PC.min_name AS a1\nFROM PreCalculated AS PC;\n```\nIn this rewritten query, we create a CTE (Common Table Expression) that calculates the minimum name once and then select from it twice. This transformation reduces the computational complexity of the original query by avoiding repeated function calls.\n\nNote: The other transformations mentioned in Rule 1 do not apply to this specific SQL query, as there are no inline functions or aggregate functions called multiple times with the same parameters.', 'time': 6.10646653175354}
